Without Malcolm McLaren, I'd have never even have got out of Glasgow. He was absolutely instrumental in creating punk rock and was the reason people like me, Bobby Gillespie and Noel Gallagher ever got into music. When I managed the Jesus and Mary Chain I lived out my Malcolm McLaren infatuation. Everything I did was 100% based on The Great Rock'n'roll Swindle, such as selling Warners the publishing without them hearing the record. He was a true situationist and I loved it.
I became friends with him in 1996 when I did a Punch interview. He was an absolute visionary. He predicted everything from MP3s and the destruction of big corporations to the rise of the PC society and the dominance of the Chinese economy. He told me about this way back in 96! And one of the reasons I appeared vaguely intelligent around that time is because I'd take him out to dinner and listen to what he had to say. We went out about 20 times and he never bought dinner once, but it was worth it. This is how the conversation would go: for 15 minutes we'd talk about me, and then for two hours we'd talk about Malcolm. He'd order the two most expensive bottles of red wine in the house and then I would be informed about what was going to happen.
I used his ideas all the time. Remember when I came out and said record companies were fucked? The BPI came out and said I was talking rubbish. But the reason I knew it was true was because I was hanging out with Malcolm McLaren – and look what happened, he was right.
