Anti-truck protestors stop traffic at the intersection of Francis and Williamstown roads. Photo: Jason South
Protesters are planning another blockade of one of Melbourne's busiest truck routes after disrupting peak-hour traffic in the city's inner west today.
About 100 protesters blocked the intersection of Francis Street and Williamstown Road, Yarraville, from 8am during a protest held by the Maribyrnong Truck Action Group.
The protest was a bid to highlight the freight traffic "nightmare" that will follow the channel deepening project.
The blockade was lifted shortly before 8.30am.
Police blocked streets leading to the intersection, causing widespread delays on surrounding roads for commuter traffic.
Maribyrnong Truck Action Group spokesman Peter Knight said another protest would be held at the intersection on April 12.
"I hope that the Government realises it is actually time to do something to solve the problem and that it is time to actually create a workable transport plan for the future," he said.
"We need port trucks off our streets now �� there is no reason why the port traffic can't be using the West Gate bridge now."
The group says the Yarraville junction is already flooded with 8000 trucks a day.
The trucks use the intersection to go from Melbourne's port to the West Gate Bridge and the Western Ring Road.
Residents say they have to endure diesel fumes and noise.
'Unbearable situation'
"This is already an unbearable situation," Mr Knight told The Age yesterday.
"With channel dredging and port expansion, the number of trucks will increase exponentially."
