You don't have to book a week off work for a blast of sunshine and the seclusion of an island. Before high-speed catamarans and low-cost airlines, the following islands were costly or a slog to reach. Now you can enjoy them for a quick weekend. (All times given are local)
Formentera
Miles of white powdery beaches, this is the forgotten Balearic island. But with the launch of a fast catamaran service it is practical for a weekend away from neighbouring Ibiza.
Getting there: EasyJet (08717500100) flies to Ibiza from London Stansted. You could catch the 06.00 on say, a Friday. Flights take two hours with prices starting from £130 return including tax. Take a 10-minute taxi to the port (about €9 - £6.50) in Ibiza Town. Catamarans to Formentera (00 34 902 160180) take 25 minutes and depart hourly between 07.30 and 20.30 for £20 return. Return on the EasyJet flight on Sunday evening leaving Ibiza at 21.30.
Where to stay: Hotel Cala Saona (00 34 971 322030) has doubles from £60 per night.
Ile de Ré
Gaining a reputation as the St Tropez of France's Atlantic coast, the Ile de Ré boasts fine oysters, beaches and long stretches of pine and evergreen oak, and is so flat it cries out to be cycled on. It's a five-minute drive over a land bridge from La Rochelle, plus a £10 toll charge.
Getting there: Ryanair (0871 246 0000) flies to La Rochelle from London Stansted in one and a half hours. Prices start at £111 return including taxes. You could catch a Friday flight at 09.45. Car hire costs £40.90 per day from Europcar (0870 607 5000) including insurances and local tax. There is a return flight on Sunday at 12.45.
Where to stay: Ile de Ré boasts three hotels in the excellent Hip Hotels France guide: Le Domaine de la Baronnie, St Martin-de-Ré (00 33 5 4609 2129), a lovely guest house; Le Chat Botte, in St Clement-des-Baleines (00 33 5 4629 2193), a good restaurant with eight bedrooms; Le Senechal, Ars-en-Ré (00 33 5 4629 4042), a small recently renovated hotel. Double room prices start at €55.
Sicily
Dubbed 'Italy's least-known art city' the capital Palermo is colourful, lively and full of surprises. It has brimming street markets, a worldly beachside enclave, superb seafood and an extravaganza of rococo churches.
