Claudio Abbado has now passed the halfway stage in his second Mahler cycle for Deutsche Grammophon. What has appeared so far - the Second, Third, Fifth, Seventh and Ninth Symphonies, all of them from concerts with either the Berlin Philharmonic or Abbado's hand-picked Lucerne Festival Orchestra - suggests that if and when it is complete (though one doubts he will ever get down to performing the Eighth again) the survey will be the finest available on disc, and this outstanding new version of the Sixth only reinforces that feeling.
It might seem paradoxical to describe a work that last 80 minutes and encompasses such extremes of emotion as Mahler's most classical symphony, but formally that is what the Sixth is - a purely orchestral work that conforms to the classical four-movement template, with a massive pair of outer movements, the first a sonata-form movement and the last a vastly expanded and elaborated rondo, enclosing a central scherzo and slow movement (or, as in this performance, a slow movement and scherzo). It is the constant tension between this classical framework and the implacably tragic nature of the musical material it contains, all of it intensely biographical, that makes the Sixth such an overwhelming work, and it is a tension that Abbado understands instinctively.
Where other conductors turn the Sixth into an anguished, gut-wrenching experience, wearing its sense of personal tragedy like a badge, Abbado respects it for what it is, first and foremost an overwhelming piece of musical architecture, which derives much of its power from the inevitability and coherence of the construction.
That's not to suggest for a moment that this is an austere presentation of the musical nuts and bolts. The weight and purpose of each movement is unfailingly recognised. Abbado's determination to balance form and emotional content surely lie behind his decision to place the Andante before the scherzo (Mahler changed his mind about their order between revisions of the symphony).
