Administrative charges have been filed against a University of Florida fraternity that published a handbook with a graph of how many beers it takes to seduce honorary female members called "little sisters."
University judicial proceedings are expected to begin next week against Beta Theta Pi, a fraternity with about 80 members.
Art Sandeen, UF's vice president for student affairs, said Wednesday the charges stem from a secret 25-page booklet that contained "highly derogatory statements" about individuals and groups affiliated with the university.
The $2 publication, called Uncensored: The Four O'Clock Weekly, is the fraternity's version of National Lampoon magazine, according to some members of the fraternity.
Among other things, the booklet featured derogatory awards for various ethnic, religious and minority groups.
"It was insulting to women in part, but also to minorities," Sandeen said. "It was totally unacceptable."
Michael Dancisin, president of Beta Theta Pi, could not be reached for comment despite repeated attempts. Members reached at the fraternity house said he would be the only spokesman for the group.
The disciplinary action apparently is part of a crackdown on fraternity misconduct since a sexually related incident last year made headlines across the state.
The furor still has not died since Pi Lambda Phi fraternity was found guilty last year of hazing as a result of a 17-year-old girl's allegations that she was repeatedly raped during a rush party.
News of the handbook, generally described as degrading by university officials, drew a strong reaction from campus leaders.
"It's really sad that people would take a backward step just when you think we're making progress," said Linda Horton, president of a rape awareness group formed after the Pi Lambda Phi incident.
But Sandeen said previous incidents have nothing to do with the disciplinary action taken in this case.
"I am very concerned and upset about this matter because of the attitude conveyed in this publication," Sandeen said. "This is an educational institution and we have high standards of expectations."
Twelve little sisters were named in the "beer" chart, which supposedly depicted how easily they could be seduced.
Tom Dougan, assistant dean of student affairs, said he got a copy of the publication Jan. 21 and filed charges against the fraternity on Monday.
If found guilty, the fraternity could be thrown off campus, he said.
"We have always had rules against students intimidating each other and abusing each other," Sandeen said.
Beta Theta Pi is being charged with actions "involving sexual activities which are harassing, coercive, intimidating or abusive."
